  "reasoning": "The patient presents with three key symptoms: a persistent sour taste in the mouth, frequent hiccups, and a recurring lump in the throat. A sour/bitter taste is a classic sign of acid regurgitation, where stomach contents flow backward into the mouth. Frequent hiccups often occur due to irritation of the diaphragm and esophagus by rising stomach acid. The 'lump in the throat' sensation (globus pharyngeus) is a well-documented symptom of chronic throat irritation from acid exposure. Together, these symptoms strongly indicate gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD). This matches Reference [1] almost verbatim, which lists the exact same symptoms and provides the identical diagnosis.",
  "final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]gastroesophageal reflux disease[/DIAGNOSIS]"
}
